---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { publicPath } from '../../lib/paths';
// The book PDF ships only when the build includes it (scripts/sync-assets.mjs, MC_INCLUDE_PDF=1), like on the Reference page.
const hasPdf = existsSync(publicPath('pdf', 'dbt-bible.pdf'));
---
<Base title="How to use this site" section="none" description="How Model Citizen works: the plan, lessons, labs, mock exams, your progress file, and the practice kit for macOS and Windows.">
  <div class="wrap wrap-read about">
    <h1>How to use this site</h1>
    <p class="lead">Model Citizen is a free, unofficial course to prepare the dbt Analytics Engineering Certification (dbt Core 1.11). It follows a verified study book, chapter by chapter. You need nothing to start: open the site in Edge, Chrome, Safari or Firefox.</p>

    <section>
      <h2>Start here</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><b>Open <a href={url('')}>Today</a></b> and choose your rhythm: about 2 hours a day for 14 weeks, or faster, or slower. The site then gives you a mission every day: a short review, lessons to read, labs to do, and a quiz.</li>
        <li><b>Read a lesson.</b> Each lesson is cut into short steps. Press <i>I understood this</i> at the end of each step. Words with a dotted line show the French translation when you point at them.</li>
        <li><b>Do the labs.</b> Some labs run in your browser (SQL, and real dbt in a browser terminal). Others run on your computer, with commands for macOS, Windows PowerShell and WSL: choose your computer with the switch at the top of a lesson.</li>
        <li><b>Check yourself.</b> Every chapter ends with a quiz in the formats of the real exam. Wrong answers go to your error notebook and come back later as review cards.</li>
        <li><b>Take the mock exams.</b> Three full exams of 65 questions in 2 hours, with a timer and the real DOMC format. The book's rule: book the real exam after two timed mocks at 80% or more.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your progress stays in your browser</h2>
      <p>There is no account and no server. Everything you do (lessons, answers, mock exams, notes) is saved in this browser on this computer. Nothing is sent anywhere, and the site uses no trackers.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>To continue on another computer, open <a href={url('settings/')}>Settings</a>, press <i>Export progress file</i>, copy the file to the other computer, and press <i>Import progress file</i> there.</li>
        <li>A private window, or clearing your browser data, deletes the progress saved in this browser. Export your file from time to time to keep a copy.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>For Windows users</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Every command on this site exists for <b>Windows PowerShell</b>. Choose <i>Windows</i> with the switch at the top of a lesson, and the terminal blocks change.</li>
        <li>dbt commands are the same on every computer. Only the commands around them change (folders, the Python environment, copying files).</li>
        <li>If you prefer a Linux shell, you can use <b>WSL</b> (Windows Subsystem for Linux) and follow the macOS and Linux commands.</li>
        <li>The <a href={url('labs/')}>practice kit</a> contains a setup script for PowerShell (<code class="mc-ic">setup.ps1</code>) and one for macOS and WSL (<code class="mc-ic">setup.sh</code>). It creates your course folder, installs dbt 1.11 and builds the practice database.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
      <ul class="keys">
        <li><kbd class="mc-key">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="mc-key">K</kbd> search the course (on a Mac, <kbd class="mc-key">⌘</kbd> + <kbd class="mc-key">K</kbd> also works)</li>
        <li><kbd class="mc-key">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="mc-key">Enter</kbd> run the query or command in a workspace</li>
        <li><kbd class="mc-key">Y</kbd> / <kbd class="mc-key">N</kbd> answer YES or NO to a DOMC option</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>About this course</h2>
      <p>The content comes from <i>The dbt Analytics Engineering Bible</i>, a study book written for this certification and checked by running every lab on dbt Core 1.11.16 with DuckDB. {hasPdf
        ? <>You can download the full book as a PDF from the <a href={url('reference/')}>Reference</a> page.</>
        : <>The book itself is not published on this site: it is available from its author.</>}</p>
      <p class="disc">Unofficial study guide. Not affiliated with or endorsed by dbt Labs. dbt is a trademark of dbt Labs, Inc. The mock exams are original questions, not official exam questions.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .about { padding-block: 40px 16px; }
  .lead { font-size: var(--fs-lg); color: var(--text); }
  section { margin-top: 32px; }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3em; display: grid; gap: 10px; }
  .steps li::marker { font-weight: 800; color: var(--accent-strong); }
  ul { padding-left: 1.2em; display: grid; gap: 6px; }
  .keys { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .disc { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--fs-sm); }
</style>
